---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { apps } from '../../data/site';
const app = apps.find((item) => item.slug === 'killmyapps')!;
---
<BaseLayout title="KillMyApps" description={app.description}>
  <div class="container page">
    <p><a class="back-link" href="/projects/">← Projects</a></p>
    <header class="page-header project-detail-header"><span class="eyebrow">Android Utility</span><h1>{app.name}</h1><p>{app.tagline}</p><div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href={app.download}>Download</a><a class="button" href={app.github}>View on GitHub</a></div></header>
    <figure class="project-banner"><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dedeadend/KillMyApps/master/screenshots/KillMyAppsBanner.png" alt="KillMyApps banner" loading="eager" /></figure>
    <section class="detail-intro"><div class="detail-stat"><strong>{app.version}</strong><span>Version</span></div><div class="detail-stat"><strong>{app.minAndroid}</strong><span>Minimum version</span></div><div class="detail-stat"><strong>Root / Shizuku</strong><span>Execution layers</span></div><div class="detail-stat"><strong>{app.license}</strong><span>Open source</span></div></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">Overview</span><h2>Focused process control.</h2><p>{app.description}</p><p>The main workflow is deliberately short: choose an application and terminate it. App discovery, automation, widgets, Quick Settings, and configurable kill levels extend that workflow without turning the project into a general-purpose app manager.</p></section>
    <section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">Features</span><h2>The main tools.</h2><div class="grid"><article class="card"><h3>One-Tap Kill</h3><p>Terminate selected running applications with a single action.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Killer Engine</h3><p>Choose between Root, Shizuku, and Shizuku+ as the execution layer for the kill operation.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Kill Levels</h3><p>Choose Soft, Deep, or Killer to control how aggressively applications are terminated.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Source Mode</h3><p>Filter the running-app list by categories such as User, Launchable, or System.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Selection Mode</h3><p>Control which applications are included in the kill operation using Include or Exclude mode.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Hide Critical Apps</h3><p>Keep KillMyApps, essential system applications, and user default apps out of the visible list.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Auto Killer</h3><p>Automatically terminate running applications when the screen turns off or at a scheduled daily time. Screen-off delay can be configured up to 15 minutes, with a fallback for missed scheduled executions.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Quick Settings Tile</h3><p>Trigger the kill action directly from Android Quick Settings.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Home Screen Widgets</h3><p>Run the kill action directly from resizable 1×1 or 1×3 home screen widgets.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Search</h3><p>Quickly find applications in the running-app list, with the keyboard hiding automatically after the search action is pressed.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Material UI</h3><p>A simple, modern interface with polished animations and custom capsule-style notifications.</p></article></div></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">How It Works</span><h2>Select, filter, and terminate.</h2><p>KillMyApps displays the currently running applications on the device. Source Mode filters that list, while Include or Exclude selection controls determine which applications remain in scope.</p><p>When the kill action is triggered, KillMyApps uses the selected Killer Engine—Root, Shizuku, or Shizuku+—to perform the required system-level operation on the applications that remain within the selected scope.</p><p>The selected Kill Level—Soft, Deep, or Killer—controls how aggressively applications are terminated.</p></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">Automation</span><h2>Run it when you need it.</h2><p>Auto Killer is now a regular, more reliable feature rather than an experimental workflow. It can run when the screen turns off with a configurable delay of up to 15 minutes, or at a fixed daily time. Scheduled kills include a fallback mechanism for missed executions.</p></section>
    <section class="section screenshots-section"><span class="eyebrow">Screenshots</span><h2>The interface in use.</h2><div class="screenshot-grid"><figure><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dedeadend/KillMyApps/master/screenshots/screenshot2.png" alt="KillMyApps dark theme" loading="lazy" /></figure><figure><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dedeadend/KillMyApps/master/screenshots/screenshot3.png" alt="KillMyApps Quick Settings tile" loading="lazy" /></figure><figure><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dedeadend/KillMyApps/master/screenshots/screenshot1.png" alt="KillMyApps light theme" loading="lazy" /></figure></div></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">Design decision</span><h2>Keep the scope narrow.</h2><p>The project adds features only when they support the same core operation across the app, widgets, Quick Settings, and automation. It is not intended to replace a full application manager.</p></section>
    <section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">Release history</span><h2>Version history.</h2><div class="release-list"><div><strong>v1.6</strong><span>Added three kill levels—Soft, Deep, and Killer—for more control over termination aggressiveness; added a direct Telegram channel link in Settings; replaced the previous toast system with custom capsule-style notifications; promoted Auto Killer from experimental status with configurable screen-off delays up to 15 minutes plus a fallback for missed scheduled executions; renamed Killer Mode to Killer Engine and improved the underlying kill logic; improved search behavior, UI animations, dependencies, Gradle/build configuration, APK metadata, and general stability; dropped Android 8.0/8.1 support and moved the minimum supported version to Android 9.0 (API 28).</span></div><div><strong>v1.5</strong><span>Added Auto Killer for screen-off and fixed daily scheduling, resizable 1×1 and 1×3 home-screen widgets, and the new Shizuku+ execution option based on UserService/AIDL for compatibility with Shizuku forks and versions; added a dedicated Donate option; redesigned the main toolbar with integrated search, refreshed the app icon and animations, adjusted app-list spacing and dimensions, updated the project to target Android 17 (API 37), and reorganized and optimized the codebase for faster execution, better memory efficiency, and bug fixes.</span></div><div><strong>v1.4</strong><span>Added a Quick Settings Tile for instant background-process termination; replaced the old Excluded tab with Selection Mode supporting Include and Exclude choices; added a setting to disable fancy list animations; made Shizuku integration stable for daily use; improved Hide Critical Apps filtering to better protect default and essential system apps; added a controlled fallback to prevent zombie processes when the app terminates itself; optimized list loading, rendering, and memory use; and polished the iconography, Settings layouts, paddings, font sizes, and status-bar/top-bar handling.</span></div><div><strong>v1.3</strong><span>Added Shizuku support for the kill operation and introduced Killer Mode as a selectable execution mode in Settings; fixed crash scenarios, improved large-list loading performance, addressed a potential memory leak, and introduced a refreshed toast notification design.</span></div><div><strong>v1.2</strong><span>Added automatic refresh for the running-apps list, introduced new animations for the running-apps tab, and added a GitHub project link at the end of Settings; optimized the kill function for better speed and efficiency, improved UI color readability and margins, and applied List UI options to the Excluded list as well.</span></div><div><strong>v1.1.0</strong><span>Added a new toast style and new animations, fixed UI colors and padding, and changed the Android package name from com.deadend.killmyapps to dedeadend.killmyapps.</span></div><div><strong>v1.0.2</strong><span>Published a follow-up maintenance release with the release page pointing to the full changelog; the release diff includes README updates plus internal refinements to app initialization, toast handling, kill-related logic, and the excluded-app workflow.</span></div><div><strong>v1.0.1</strong><span>Fixed UI lag while killing all apps and changed the default app-list mode to Launcher.</span></div><div><strong>v1.0.0</strong><span>First stable release of KillMyApps.</span></div></div></section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
